.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped \- declare a ccol_event_loop with automatic destruction on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a ccol_event_loop variable with the initial value
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID. It also tags the variable with a cleanup
attribute. The library therefore destroys the loop automatically when
the variable leaves its scope, exactly as ccol_event_loop_destroy()
does. You must still assign the variable before you use it. Use
ccol_event_loop_construct(), or a ccol_event_loop_create*() call.

Prefer ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped() when the declaration and the
initialisation can happen together. That is the common case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses
__attribute__((cleanup)).
It is safe to leave the variable at CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID for the
whole scope. The cleanup does nothing for that value.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped(loop);
    char *err = NULL;
    loop = ccol_event_loop_create(32, 1, 1, &err);
    if (!loop) { fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", err); return; }
    /* ... */
}  /* the library destroys loop automatically here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_declare (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3)
